# MeetingScribe: zero-touch, on-device Teams meeting notes

MeetingScribe runs in the background, notices when you join a Microsoft Teams call, and records it as two
tracks: your microphone (**Me**) and Teams' own audio output (everyone else). When the call ends it:

1. transcribes both tracks with Whisper,
2. tells the remote participants apart by voice and recognizes people it has heard before,
3. merges everything by timestamp,
4. has a local LLM drop everything that isn't work (small talk, personal conversation),
5. has a local LLM write a summary and action items from the work discussion,
6. saves a Markdown file to `~/Documents/Meeting Notes/`.

Nothing leaves the Mac, and you never need to start or stop it. Optionally, `./ctl.sh jira` then turns the
meeting's work into Jira tickets or comments, each one only after you say yes (see *Pushing action items to Jira*).

A narrated walkthrough (about 3½ minutes) is in `MeetingScribe_Demo.mp4`; `video/` holds its source (see *Demo video*).

```
Teams call starts ──► Core Audio: Teams process is using the mic (macOS 14.4+ process objects)
                        │  (start_after_s debounce)
                        ▼
                     record  mic.pcm    ◄── microphone (sounddevice / Core Audio)
                             system.pcm ◄── Teams audio only (Core Audio process tap, audio-only)
                        │
Teams call ends ───► mic released for stop_after_s ─► stop
                        ▼
              process (sub-process, low priority)
                 energy-gate silence → mlx-whisper (per track) → echo removal
                 → voice embeddings (ECAPA) → cluster speakers → match voiceprint library
                 → chronological merge → work-only filter → Qwen 2.5 14B via mlx-lm (or Ollama on 127.0.0.1) → Markdown
```

## Disclaimer

MeetingScribe is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. **You alone are responsible for how you use it**:
for telling everyone in a meeting that it is being recorded and getting their consent where required, for
consent to create and keep voiceprints (biometric data), for anything you push to Jira, and for following every
law, regulation and company policy that applies to you and the people you record (for example call-recording
consent laws, GDPR, BIPA and your employer's rules). **The developer, and the developer's employer, are not
responsible or liable** for your use of MeetingScribe or for any loss, claim or damage that results from it.
MeetingScribe is not an official tool of any company, and nothing here is legal advice.

`./install.sh` shows these terms and installs only after you type `I agree`; the background agent doesn't record
and `./ctl.sh jira` doesn't run until they are accepted (`./ctl.sh terms` shows them again).

## Requirements

* Apple Silicon Mac on macOS 14.4 or later (developed on macOS 27.2, M4 Max)
* About 8 GB free disk for the models, plus about 230 MB per hour of meeting while it is being processed
* No admin rights, no virtual audio drivers (BlackHole, Soundflower and the like are **not** used)

## 1. Python environment

Homebrew's Python works. Use **3.12 to 3.14**, because MLX and numba don't publish wheels for 3.15 yet.

```bash
cd "~/Desktop/AdHoc Developments/Meeting Tracker"     # or wherever this folder lives
/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install --upgrade pip
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
```

No Homebrew? [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) installs into your home folder without admin:
`curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh && uv venv -p 3.12 .venv && uv pip install -p .venv -r requirements.txt`.

## 2. Download the models (the only step that uses the network)

```bash
.venv/bin/python meeting_scribe.py download-models
```

This fetches three models into the Hugging Face cache:

* `whisper-large-v3-turbo` (1.6 GB)
* the SpeechBrain ECAPA voice-embedding model (80 MB)
* `Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-4bit` (8.3 GB): summary, action items and the work-only filter

From then on every run sets `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, so models load only from disk.

**Optional, to use Ollama instead of mlx-lm:** set `llm_backend = "ollama"` in `config.toml`, then:

```bash
brew services start ollama        # listens on 127.0.0.1:11434 only
ollama pull qwen2.5:14b           # or another local model
```

The script refuses any Ollama URL that isn't loopback.

## 3. Install the background agent

```bash
./install.sh
```

This creates:

| What | Where | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `MeetingScribe.app` | `~/Applications/` | A tiny AppleScript applet built with the stock `osacompile`. It launches the daemon, so macOS permissions are granted to *MeetingScribe* and not to Terminal or a raw `python` binary. Neither the app nor its Python process shows a Dock icon. |
| Launch agent | `~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.meetingscribe.plist` | Starts the app at login and restarts it within about 10 s if it ever exits. |
| Logs | `~/Library/Logs/MeetingScribe/` | `daemon.log` (detection and recording), `process.log` (transcription, speakers, summary) |

## 4. Grant permissions (one time)

| Permission | Needed? | How |
|---|---|---|
| **Files and Folders → Desktop** | Yes, if this folder lives on the Desktop. | A prompt saying *"MeetingScribe would like to access files in your Desktop folder"* appears on first start. Click **Allow**. |
| **Microphone** | Yes, for the "Me" track. | A prompt appears on first start. Click **Allow**. (The Microphone pane has no **+** button, so the prompt is the only way in. If you dismissed it, run `tccutil reset Microphone local.meetingscribe` then `./ctl.sh restart`.) |
| **System Audio Recording Only** | Yes, for Teams' audio. | Prompted at the first call. If it doesn't appear: **Privacy & Security → Screen & System Audio Recording → System Audio Recording Only**, switch on **MeetingScribe**. |
| **Screen Recording** | Optional. It is used only to read the Teams window title, which becomes the note's title; otherwise notes are titled "Teams meeting". Nothing on your screen is captured. | Same pane, upper section. Click **+**, press <kbd>⌘⇧G</kbd>, type `~/Applications/MeetingScribe.app`, then **Open**. |
| Accessibility | **No** | Nothing synthesizes input or reads other apps' UI. |
| Automation | **No** | The only AppleScript used is `display notification`, which needs no Automation grant. |

After changing a permission, run `./ctl.sh restart`. Re-running `install.sh` re-signs the app, and macOS then
treats it as a new app, so you'll be asked for these permissions again.

## 5. Verify with a Teams test call

1. In Teams, open **Settings → Devices → Make a test call**.
2. In a second terminal, run `./ctl.sh logs`. You should see `meeting detected … recording session …`, then
   `process tap running …`, and a notification.
3. Hang up. Shortly after, you'll see `meeting ended`.
4. Notes appear in `~/Documents/Meeting Notes/YYYY-MM-DD HHMM - <meeting title>.md`.

The Echo test bot plays your own message back to you, so in a test call your words also show up under the
remote side. That is real playback audio, and it doesn't happen in real meetings.

## Day-to-day commands

```bash
./ctl.sh status     # is it running? what does it detect right now?
./ctl.sh logs       # live log
./ctl.sh restart    # after editing config.toml
./ctl.sh stop       # pause until next `./ctl.sh start` (or next login)
./ctl.sh start

./ctl.sh name "Speaker 2" "Priya Shah"    # name a voice in the latest meeting (see below)
./ctl.sh voices                           # list known voices
./ctl.sh voices --forget "Priya Shah"     # delete a voiceprint

./ctl.sh jira                             # review the latest meeting's work and push it to Jira

./uninstall.sh      # remove app and agent; notes are kept

# Retry a session whose processing failed (its raw audio is kept until processing succeeds):
.venv/bin/python meeting_scribe.py process ~/Library/Application\ Support/MeetingScribe/sessions/<id>
```

## Identifying speakers

Everyone on the remote side arrives mixed into one audio stream, so MeetingScribe tells them apart by voice:

1. Every 1 s it takes a 2 s slice of speech and computes a voice fingerprint for it (a 192-number ECAPA-TDNN
   embedding, running on-device).
2. Slices whose fingerprints are similar are grouped into one speaker.
3. Each transcribed sentence is given the speaker who owns most of it.
4. Each speaker is compared against your **voiceprint library**
   (`~/Library/Application Support/MeetingScribe/voices.json`). A match uses the stored name; anyone else
   becomes *Speaker 1*, *Speaker 2*, and so on.

**Teaching it names.** The first time someone new is heard, the note lists them as *(unrecognized)* and
adds an **Unrecognized voices** table showing when each one first spoke and what they said. If that
doesn't tell you who it was, listen to them:

```bash
./ctl.sh listen                 # plays a ~10 s clip of each unknown voice in the latest meeting,
                                # with their first lines printed alongside
./ctl.sh listen "Speaker 2"     # just one voice
```

Clips are kept even with `keep_audio = false`, but only for voices that still need a name. A clip is
deleted as soon as you name that voice. Then:

```bash
./ctl.sh name "Speaker 2" "Priya Shah"            # latest meeting
./ctl.sh name "Speaker 1" "Tom Berg" 2026-10-28   # a specific meeting (session id or date)
```

This rewrites that meeting's note (participants, summary, action items and transcript) and saves Priya's
voiceprint. From the next meeting on, Priya is labeled automatically. Every confident match refines her
voiceprint a little, so recognition improves over time.

* **Wrong person recognized?** Run `./ctl.sh name "Wrong Name" "Right Name"` on that meeting to fix the note,
  then `./ctl.sh voices --forget "Wrong Name"` if that voiceprint is polluted.
* **One person split into two speakers?** Name both labels with the same name; the note and voiceprint merge.
* **Several people in one meeting room** sharing a microphone are still told apart, because it's by voice.
* **Tuning:** `speaker_cluster_threshold` (default 0.6) controls how readily slices are grouped; lower it if
  two people get merged into one speaker. `speaker_match_threshold` (default 0.5) is how similar a voice must
  be to reuse a stored name. Set `speaker_id = false` to label everyone "Others" as before.
* **Too many speakers?** Fragments are cleaned up automatically: clusters whose voices are at least
  `speaker_merge_similarity` (default 0.7) alike are merged, and any voice with less than
  `speaker_min_seconds` (default 15) of talk time is folded into the closest voice. Raise
  `speaker_min_seconds` if scraps still show up, or set `max_speakers` as a hard cap (0 = no cap).

On synthetic test voices, the same voice scored 0.83–0.90 against its stored voiceprint, and different voices
never scored above 0.51. Real Teams audio is compressed and noisier, so expect somewhat lower scores.

## Output

```markdown
# Migration sync
**Date:** Wednesday 28 October 2026, 14:00–14:47 (47 min)
**Participants:** Me, Samantha Lee, Daniel Ortiz, Speaker 3 (unrecognized)

## Summary
- Database migration finished; tables validated.
- ...

## Action Items
- [ ] Me: Update the reporting jobs by Thursday
- [ ] Speaker 3: Raise the firewall change with the security team today

---
## Transcript
> To name a voice (and recognize it automatically from now on): `./ctl.sh name "Speaker 3" "Full Name"`

**[00:00:00] Me:** Good morning everyone, let's go through the migration status
**[00:00:05] Samantha Lee:** The database migration finished last night...
```

With `keep_audio = true`, a stereo WAV (left = Me, right = remote side) is saved next to each note. Raw
session audio is deleted once processing succeeds; voiceprints are kept, but no audio.

## How detection and capture work

* **Start:** Since macOS 14.4, Core Audio exposes every process that uses audio as a *process object*, with
  "is running input" and "is running output" flags. MeetingScribe polls these once per second, without
  opening any device itself. When a `com.microsoft.teams2.*` process (in practice the
  `com.microsoft.teams2.modulehost` call engine) holds the microphone for `start_after_s`, recording starts.
  Audio before that point is not captured.
* **Capture:** Teams' audio is captured with a **Core Audio process tap**. This is a private, invisible tap
  on the Teams processes only, wrapped in a private aggregate device. It is audio-only, so macOS shows no
  screen-recording glow, and you keep hearing the meeting normally.
* **Stop:** Teams releases the mic when you leave the call. After `stop_after_s` with no Teams mic use,
  recording stops and processing starts. If Teams releases the mic while you're muted, an open meeting window
  keeps the recording alive for up to 10 minutes, but only while Teams still has call audio open.
* **Robustness:**
  * Switching headsets mid-call restarts mic capture.
  * If the Teams-only tap stays silent while Teams is playing audio, it falls back to tapping all system audio.
  * A crash or power loss mid-call keeps everything recorded so far (raw PCM), and it is processed on the
    next start.
  * Recordings shorter than `min_meeting_s` are discarded.
  * A hung Core Audio call can't freeze the daemon, because streams are closed with a timeout.

## Work-only notes

With `work_only = true` (the default), the local LLM reads the transcript in numbered batches and lists the
lines that aren't about work: weekends, holidays, family, health, food, sports, weather, jokes and chit-chat
while waiting for people to join. Those lines are discarded before anything is written, so they don't appear
in the summary, the note's transcript, `transcript.json` or the voice-sample quotes. The note's header says
how many lines were left out (`**Filtered:** 12 non-work line(s) left out`). Only the count is logged.

* It runs in two passes on **Qwen 2.5 14B**, the same model that writes the summary (loaded once). First the model lists candidate
  lines batch by batch. Then each candidate is re-checked with the three lines around it, and it's dropped
  only if the model calls it a greeting/goodbye or personal small talk. Llama 3.1 8B was tried first, but it
  dropped terse work lines such as "What did the DBS say?". To filter with a different mlx model than the
  summary, set `work_filter_model`.
* Meeting logistics ("can you see my screen") and short replies ("yes", "okay") to work points are kept.
* When the model is unsure, it keeps the line. If its answer for a batch can't be read, the whole batch is kept.
* Discarded lines can't be recovered. With `keep_audio = true` the WAV still holds the full call.
* To filter meetings processed before this existed: `./ctl.sh refilter` (all unfiltered meetings) or
  `./ctl.sh refilter --session 2026-11-03_0832`. It rewrites `transcript.json` and the note, including the
  summary. Add `--force` to run it again on meetings already filtered.
* Set `work_only = false` to keep every line.

## Pushing action items to Jira

`./ctl.sh jira` turns the latest meeting's work into Jira tickets, with you approving each one. It is the
only part of MeetingScribe that uses the network, it runs only when you start it, and it talks only to
your own Jira site. The drafting and the duplicate check use the same local model as the notes.

```bash
./ctl.sh jira                          # latest meeting
./ctl.sh jira --session 2026-11-05     # a specific meeting
./ctl.sh jira --project EDA            # skip the space question
./ctl.sh jira --dry-run                # search and draft, but write nothing to Jira
./ctl.sh jira --refresh                # re-read the meeting and review every item again
./ctl.sh jira --forget                 # delete the saved token / cookie from the Keychain
```

What happens:

1. **Sign in.** Set `[jira].base_url` in `config.toml` (or type it when asked). Then choose how to sign in
   and paste the secret; input is hidden:
   * **Personal access token** for Jira Server / Data Center,
   * **API token + email** for Jira Cloud,
   * **Browser session cookie** for SSO-only sites: open Jira in your browser while signed in, open the
     developer tools' Network tab, reload, click any request to your Jira site and copy the whole `Cookie`
     request header.

   After a successful sign-in it is stored in your macOS login Keychain (service "MeetingScribe Jira") through
   the Security framework, never in a file or on a command line. When it expires, Jira rejects it, the stored
   copy is deleted and you're asked for a fresh one. `./ctl.sh jira --forget` removes it. `base_url` must
   start with `https://`; anything else is refused so the secret is always encrypted in transit.
2. **Pick the space.** The spaces (projects) you can see are listed; you type the key new tickets go to.
   The last one used is suggested.
3. **Extract.** The model reads the note and transcript and lists the work that belongs in Jira: title,
   details, owner, due date (resolved against the meeting date), type, priority, search keywords, and the
   transcript line it came from. The list is saved in the session's `jira.json`, so a re-run reviews the
   same items.
4. **Search every space.** For each item, ticket keys mentioned in the meeting are fetched, and a text
   search runs across *all* spaces you can see (tickets updated in the last `search_days`). The best
   `search_limit` matches go to the model, which labels each one *same work*, *related* or *unrelated*,
   and says whether the meeting reported the problem as back again.
5. **Decide.** From those labels:
   * same work, ticket still open → suggest a **comment** on it with the meeting's update (no duplicate),
   * same work, ticket Done, problem not back → suggest **skipping** it,
   * same work but Done and the problem is back, or no match → **create** a ticket, linked ("Relates") to the
     matching and related tickets.
6. **Draft.** The space's create screen is read (`createmeta`), and the model fills summary, description
   (Context / What needs doing / Acceptance criteria, in Jira wiki markup), priority, due date, labels,
   components and any *required* custom fields, choosing only values the space allows. The owner becomes
   the assignee when Jira finds that person ("Me" is you; "Speaker N" stays unassigned).
7. **You decide.** Every comment and ticket is shown in full and waits for **y** (push), **n** (skip) or
   **e** (edit any field, the type or the assignee; the description opens in `$EDITOR`). A required field
   the model couldn't fill is marked ⚠ and must be set before it can be pushed.

Afterwards the note gets a `## Jira` section linking every ticket created or commented on, and `jira.json`
records them, so running it again won't push the same item twice. When `base_url` is set, the "notes
ready" notification reminds you to run `./ctl.sh jira`.

A test against a mock Jira with four action items gave: two comments on open tickets that already covered
the work (one in another space), one new ticket linked to a related one, and one new ticket for a problem
whose Done ticket had come back. Always read the draft: the model can still pick the wrong due date or
guess a required field.

## Transcript quality

* **Apart from the work-only filter, nothing is deleted from the transcript.** Lines that look suspicious are kept and tagged instead:
  * _(possible echo of the remote side)_: on speakers, your mic also hears the other side. A line on your
    mic that overlaps in time with, and reads like, a remote line gets this tag.
  * _(low confidence)_ or _(possible mis-hearing)_: Whisper wasn't sure, or produced one of its typical
    phrases for noise ("Thank you.", "Thanks for watching").

  Each tagged line is also logged in `process.log`. Set `drop_suspect_lines = true` to delete them instead.
* Long silences are skipped before Whisper runs, which is faster and stops Whisper from inventing text in
  muted stretches. The silence threshold is lenient and leaves 1 s of padding around anything that sounds
  like speech.

## Privacy

* Audio is captured by Core Audio in-process and written only to `~/Library/Application Support/MeetingScribe/`.
* Whisper and the LLM run on the GPU through MLX, or through Ollama bound to 127.0.0.1. Voice embeddings run
  on the CPU through PyTorch.
* Voiceprints are 192 numbers per person, stored locally in `voices.json`. You can't reconstruct audio from
  them, but they are biometric data, so treat the file accordingly.
* `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1` and `HF_HUB_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1` are set before any ML library
  is imported.
* To check for yourself: during processing, `nettop -p $(pgrep -f "meeting_scribe.py process")` shows no
  connections.

**Consent:** many jurisdictions and employers require all participants to agree to being recorded, and
voiceprints are biometric data with their own rules (for example under GDPR or Illinois' BIPA). Check your
company's policy and tell participants.

## Configuration

Edit `config.toml`, then run `./ctl.sh restart`. Useful keys:

* `my_name`: the label used for your lines
* `language`: e.g. `"en"`, to skip auto-detection
* `mic_device`: e.g. `"AirPods"`
* `start_after_s`, `stop_after_s`, `min_meeting_s`: detection timing
* `capture_mode = "all"`: tap all system audio, if the remote side is silent on your Teams build
* `system_audio_backend = "screencapturekit"`: the older capture path (shows the orange glow)
* `speaker_id`, `speaker_cluster_threshold`, `speaker_match_threshold`, `speaker_merge_similarity`,
  `speaker_min_seconds`, `max_speakers`: see *Identifying speakers*
* `whisper_model`: e.g. `"mlx-community/whisper-small-mlx"`, for older or 8 GB Macs
* `mlx_llm_model`: e.g. `"mlx-community/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-4bit"`

Run `download-models` again after changing a model.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| `daemon: NOT running` | Check `~/Library/Logs/MeetingScribe/stderr.log`. Re-run `./install.sh` after moving this folder or recreating `.venv`. |
| Daemon never logs `daemon started` after a reinstall | A permission prompt (usually Desktop folder access) is waiting on screen. |
| `microphone delivers pure silence` | Microphone permission was denied; see step 4. |
| `system audio is pure silence even when tapping everything` | "System Audio Recording Only" permission was denied; see step 4. |
| Remote side silent | Set `capture_mode = "all"`. |
| Meeting not detected | Run `./ctl.sh status` during a call; `Teams mic in use` should be `True`. |
| Notes titled "Teams meeting" | Grant the optional Screen Recording permission so the window title can be read. |
| Two remote people show as one speaker | Lower `speaker_cluster_threshold` (e.g. 0.5); it applies from the next meeting. |

## Demo video

`MeetingScribe_Demo.mp4` (1080p, about 3½ minutes, narrated and captioned) is built on this Mac from `video/`:

* `script.json`: the narration, one entry per scene. Edit the words here; scene lengths follow the speech.
* `motion.html`: the animation. Open it in a browser to preview (space = pause, ← → = seek 5 s).
* `build_video.py`: speaks the narration with Kokoro-82M (an open, Apache-2.0 neural voice that runs offline
  from `video/models/`), renders every frame in an off-screen WebKit view,
  and encodes with the ffmpeg bundled in `imageio-ffmpeg`.

```bash
/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14 -m venv video/.venv
video/.venv/bin/pip install imageio-ffmpeg pyobjc-framework-WebKit pyobjc-framework-Quartz kokoro-onnx
# once: put kokoro-v1.0.onnx and voices-v1.0.bin (github.com/thewh1teagle/kokoro-onnx releases) in video/models/
video/.venv/bin/python video/build_video.py                    # about 10 minutes
video/.venv/bin/python video/build_video.py --stills 30,120    # PNG frames to check a change quickly
```

People, meetings and ticket numbers in the video are made up.
